  "worked_solution": "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)+3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+3{\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{3}{2}({3}^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{27}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
